                          Chapter 1, Starting Out
                      1.1 - Creating a New Character

        If you are in the main game Guild, then select the S)elect
Character command. It will load up the character generator. Once inside,
select R)egister a new character. It will ask you which race you want your
character to be. (Refer to 2.1 - Races for more information) Next you will
be asked what alignment you want your character to be. (Refer to 2.2 -
Alignments for more information) Then it will roll your stats (refer to 2.4
for more information) and list all the possible classes you may be. Select
one. (Refer to 2.3 - Classes for more information) Then select a name for
your character. After this, it will ask if everything is okay. If you are
satisified, select Y)es. If you want to roll again, select N)o and repeat
the above directions until you are satisfied with a character.

                        1.2 - Selecting a Character

        To select a character you wish to use, enter the character
generator (S)elect Character command in the Guild), and select the S)elect
Character command. It will list all the characters you currently have.
Enter the corresponding number, and you now have that character selected to
use. To play, use the Q)uit back to "Dragon's Lord" command.

                        1.3 - Deleting a Character

        To delete a character, select the D)elete Character command from
the Character Generator. This will display a list of characters you have
already created. Simply select the number of the character you want to
delete, and hit Y)es. That character is now deleted. **Warning** Once you
delete a character, there is no getting it back. It is forever gone. Be
sure this is what you want to do before you delete it.


                  Chapter 2, Character Classes and Races
                                2.1 - Races

        There are five races in Dragon's Lord. Below is a list of each, and
a brief synopsis of their abilities.

 Human: The most common race. The human is average in everything except
        Wisdom, where it lacks.

   Elf: A tall slender race. The Elf makes a great magic user, as it
        generally has very high Intelligence. Elves are also decent
        fighters, as they have high Dexterity.

 Dwarf: Dwarves are short creatures. They tend to have high Strength and
        high Constitution. These make the best fighters.

 Gnome: Gnomes are cave dwelling creatures. Gnomes tend to have high
        Wisdom, and usually make the best Clerics.

Hobbit: Hobbits are short dexterious creatures. Hobbits are the best
        thieves because they tend to have the highest Dexterity and Luck.

        By no means does the race limit you to what class you can be. These
are just normalities for each race. Sometimes a strange race can offer new
advantages to an otherwise "dull" class.

                              2.2, Alignments

        There are three alignments in Dragon's Lord.

   Good: These characters would go out of their way to help an old lady
         cross a street.

Neutral: These characters would help an old lady across the street if they
         were going the same way.

   Evil: These characters would help an old lady cross a street only if
         there was some major gain to them, such as a reward.

        Alignments don't always restrict your character, but they do have a
role in which class they may become. (See 2.3, Classes for more
information)

